","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we need to use the following equation:

ΔS = ΣnΔS(products) - ΣnΔS(reactants)

where n is the number of moles and ΔS is the change in entropy per mole.

For the reaction:

3CO(g) + 1.5O2(g) → 3CO2(g)

We need the values of ΔS(products) and ΔS(reactants) for each gas. The standard entropy values (ΔS°) for CO, O2, and CO2 at 298 K are as follows:

ΔS°(CO) = 188.7 J/mol·K
ΔS°(O2) = 205.0 J/mol·K
ΔS°(CO2) = 215.8 J/mol·K

Now we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [3 × ΔS°(CO2)] - [3 × ΔS°(CO) + 1.5 × ΔS°(O2)]

ΔS = [3 × 215.8 J/mol·K] - [3 × 188.7 J/mol·K + 1.5 × 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [647.4 J/mol·K] - [573.1 J/mol·K + 307.5 J/mol•K]

ΔS = 647.4 J/mol·K - 880.6 J/mol·K

ΔS = -233.2 J/mol·K

