","When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is passed into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \texttext{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the sulfides formed will precipitate if their solubility product (\( K_{sp} \)) is greater than the solubility product of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) in the solution. The solubility product of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) is very small in acidic solutions, making the solubilities of its sulfides very low. 

Thus, the sulfides of these metal ions will precipitate as:
1. \( \text{HgS} \) (mercury sulphide): \( \text{Hg}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{HgS} \) (white precipitate)
2. \( \text{CuS} \) (cadium sulphide): \( \text{Cu}^{2+} + \text{S^{2-}} \rightarrow \text{CuS} \) (white precipitate)
3. \( \text{MnS} \) (mermium sulphide): \( \text{Mn}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{MnS} \) (white precipitate)

The solubility product of \( \text{Na}_2\text{SO}_4} \) is higher than \( \text{HgS} \), \( \text{CuS} \), and \( \text{MnS} \), so \( \text{Na}_2\text{SO}_4} \) does not precipitate. 

Thus, the correct answer is:  
\[ \boxed{\text{(a) HgS, CuS, MnS}} \]",fluency,"# Task Description
