","The primary structure of a protein refers to the linear sequence of amino acids in the protein chain. It is the simplest sequence of amino acids that make up the protein, and it is encoded by the genetic information in the DNA. The amino acids are linked together by peptide bonds, which are formed between the amino group of one amino acid and the carboxyl group of another.

The primary structure is represented as a sequence of the amino acids' three-letter or one-letter abbreviations. For example, a small polypeptide chain may have a primary structure of:

\[
\text{Gly-Gly- Leu-Lys-Lys-Lys-Lys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-clys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cys-cgt
\]

This notation indicates that the amino acid glycine (Gly) is at the first position, glycine at the second position, leucine (Leu) at the third position, and so on.

