   - $\cos (180^\circ + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$
   - $\sin (\alpha + 360^\circ) = \sin \alpha$
   - $\sin (-\alpha - 180^\circ) = -\sin (\alpha + 180^\circ) = -\sin \alpha$
   - $\cos (-180^\circ - \alpha) = -\cos (\alpha + 180^\circ) = \cos \alpha$

   Substituting these into the expression:
   \[
   \frac{\cos (180^\circ + \alpha) \cdot \sin (\alpha + 360^\circ)}{\sin (-\alpha - 180^\circ) \cdot \cos (-180^\circ - \alpha)} = \frac{(-1 \cdot \cos \alpha) \cdot \sin \alpha}{(-\sin \alpha) \cdot \cos \alpha} = \frac{-\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha}{-1 \cdot \sin \alpha \cdot \cos \alpha} = 1.
