","1. **Thermal de Broglie wavelength (\(\lambda\))**:
   The thermal de Broglie wavelength is given by:
   \[
   \lambda = \frac{h}{m \sqrt{k_B T}}
   \]
   For \(^4\text{He}\):
   - \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}\) (Planck's constant)
   - \(m = 4 \times 1.663 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg} = 6.72 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg}\) (mass of \(^4\text{He}\))
   - \(k_B = 1.380 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K}\) (Boltzmann constant)
   - \(T = 290 \text{ K}\)

   Substituting:
   \[
   \lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{6.72 \times 10^{-27} \times \sqrt{1.380 \times 10^{-23} \times 290}} = 1.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m}
   \]

2. **Free Helmholtz factor (\(\exp(\mu/kT)\))**:
   For an ideal gas, the mean-squared speed \(v^2\) is:
   \[
   v^2 = \frac{2RT}{m} = \frac{2RT}{m}
   \]
   The mean-square mean-squared velocity is:
   \[
   \langle v^2 \rangle = \frac{2kT}{m}
   \]
   The mean-square mean-squared pressure is:
   \[
   \langle p \rangle = \frac{kT}{m}
   \]
   The Helmholtz distribution distribution is:
   \[
   \exp(\mu/kT} = \frac{1}{m} \sqrt{\frac{2\pi k_B T}{m}}
   \]
   Substituting:
   \[
   \exp(\mu/kT) = \frac{1}{6.72 \times 10^{-27}} \sqrt{\frac{2 \times 1.380 \times 10^{-23} \times 290}{6.72 \times 10^{-27}}} = 1.10 \times 10^{10}
   \]

