","The decay length (\(\lambda_d\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{1}{\lambda} \sin^{-1} \left( \frac{n_f}{n_{\text{eff}}} \right)
\]  
where:  
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu m\) (wavelength of the incident light).  
- \(\lambda_d\) is the decay length.  
- \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective index of the waveguide.  
- \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the film (or substrate) of the waveguide.  

Given \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), we can rewrite this as \(n_{\text{eff}} = n_f + 0.3\). Substituting this into the formula:  
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{1}{\lambda} \sin^{-1} \left( \frac{n_f}{n_f + 0.3} \right)
\]  

The exact value of \(\lambda_d\) depends on \(n_f\). For most thin layers (e.g., silicon oxide or silicon nitride), \(n_f \approx 1.3\). Substituting \(n_f = 1.3\):  
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{1}{1000 \, \mu m} \sin^{-1} \left( \frac{1.3}{1.3 + 0.3} \right) = \frac{1}{1000 \, \mu m} \sin^{-1} \left( \frac{1.3}{1.6} \right)
\]  
\[
